<problem>
<!-- <title>critical points of exp(cubic)</title> -->

<setup>

<selectFromSequence name="a" from="-6" to=" 6" exclude="0" />
<selectFromSequence name="b" from="-5" to=" 5" numToSelect="2" />

<selectFromSequence name="d" from="-10" to=" 10" />



<function name="cubic" variables="x" symbolic="true"><math simplify="true">$a /3*x^3-$a /2*($b[1] +$b[2] )*x^2+$a *$b[1] *$b[2] *x+$d</math> </function>


<derivative name="dcubic" variables="x">$cubic</derivative>
<function name="fun" variables="x">e^($$cubic(x))</function>
<derivative name="fun_prime" variables="x">$fun</derivative>
<solveEquations name="solveForRoots">$$dcubic(x)=0</solveEquations>
<p><mathList name="critical_points">$solveForRoots.solutions</mathList></p>
<math extend="$critical_points[1]" name="cp1" />
<math extend="$critical_points[2]" name="cp2" />
<subsetOfReals name="interval1">(-Infinity,$cp1 )</subsetOfReals>
<subsetOfReals name="interval2">($cp1 ,$cp2 )</subsetOfReals>
<subsetOfReals name="interval3">($cp2 ,Infinity)</subsetOfReals>
<math name="auxpoint1" simplify="true">floor($cp1 -1)</math>
<math name="auxpoint3" simplify="true">ceil($cp2 +1)</math>
<math name="auxpoint2a" simplify="numbers">($cp1 +$cp2 )/2</math>
<math name="auxpoint2b" simplify="true">floor($auxpoint2a )</math>
<conditionalContent name="auxpoint2c">
  <case condition="$auxpoint2b  >$cp1">
    $auxpoint2b
  </case>
  <else><math>ceil($auxpoint2a)</math></else>
</conditionalContent>
<conditionalContent name="auxpoint2">
  <case condition="$auxpoint2c[1][1]  &lt; $cp2">
    $auxpoint2c[1][1]
  </case>
  <else>$auxpoint2a</else>
</conditionalContent>

<math name="fun_prime_a1" simplify="full">$$fun_prime($auxpoint1 )</math>
<math name="fun_prime_a2" simplify="full">$$fun_prime($auxpoint2[1][1] )</math>
<math name="fun_prime_a3" simplify="full">$$fun_prime($auxpoint3 )</math>
<conditionalContent name="increasedecrease1">
  <case condition="$fun_prime_a1 >0">
    <text>increasing</text>
  </case>
  <else><text>decreasing</text></else>
</conditionalContent>
<conditionalContent name="increasedecrease2">
  <case condition="$fun_prime_a2 >0">
    <text>increasing</text>
  </case>
  <else><text>decreasing</text></else>
</conditionalContent>
<conditionalContent name="increasedecrease3">
  <case condition="$fun_prime_a3 >0">
    <text>increasing</text>
  </case>
  <else><text>decreasing</text></else>
</conditionalContent>

</setup>


<p>Let <m>f(x) =<math extend="$fun.formula" /></m>.</p>



<exercise>
<p>Calculate <m>f'(x)</m>.</p>

<p><m>f'(x) =</m> 
<answer>
<award>$fun_prime </award>
</answer>
</p>



</exercise>

<exercise>
<p>Find the critical points of <m>f</m>. </p>

<p>Critical points = 
<answer>
<mathInput name="micps" />
<award matchPartial="true" unorderedCompare="true">
  <when>$micps = $critical_points</when>
</award>
</answer>
  (If there are multiple answers, separate by commas; do not round.)</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<p>The critical points divide the number line into three intervals.  What are these three intervals?  Enter them from left to right.</p>

<p>Interval 1 =
<answer>
  <mathInput name="mi1" />
  <award referencesAreResponses="$mi1">
    <when><subsetOfReals>$mi1</subsetOfReals> = $interval1</when>
  </award>
</answer>
</p>
<p>Interval 2 =
<answer>
  <mathInput name="mi2" />
  <award referencesAreResponses="$mi2">
    <when><subsetOfReals>$mi2</subsetOfReals> = $interval2</when>
  </award>
</answer>
</p>
<p>Interval 3 =
<answer>
  <mathInput name="mi3" />
  <award referencesAreResponses="$mi3">
    <when><subsetOfReals>$mi3</subsetOfReals> = $interval3</when>
  </award>
</answer>
</p>


<hint>
You can write the intervals as <m>(a,b)</m> or as <m>a &lt; x &lt; b</m>.  Or if an interval extends to infinity, you can write it, for example, as <m>(-\infty, b)</m> or <m>x &lt; b</m>.
</hint>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<p>On each of these intervals, <m>f'(x)</m> does not change sign.  Pick an auxiliary point in each interval, test the sign of <m>f'(x)</m> at that point, and conclude whether <m>f</m> is increasing or decreasing on that interval.  Enter either <alert>increasing</alert> or <alert>decreasing</alert> in the answer blanks.</p>

<p>On interval 1, <m>f</m> is 
<answer type="text">
<award>$increasedecrease1[1][1] </award>
</answer>
</p>
<p>On interval 2, <m>f</m> is 
<answer type="text">
<award>$increasedecrease2[1][1] </award>
</answer>
</p>
<p>On interval 3, <m>f</m> is 
<answer type="text">
<award>$increasedecrease3[1][1] </award>
</answer>
</p>
</exercise>

<solution>
<exercise>
<m>f'(x) = <math extend="$fun_prime.formula" /></m>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<p>The derivative always exists, so the critical points are where <m>f'(x)=0</m>.</p>

<p>To solve <me><math extend="$fun_prime.formula" />=0,</me>
we observe that the exponential can never be zero.  The only way for the <m>f'(x)</m> to be zero is if
<me><math extend="$dcubic.formula" />=0.</me>
We can solve this condition using the quadratic formula.  The results are the critical points <m>$cp1 </m> and <m>$cp2 </m>.</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
The intervals are: <m>(-\infty, $cp1 )</m>, <m>($cp1 ,$cp2 )</m>, and <m>($cp2 ,\infty)</m>.
</exercise>

<exercise>
<p>For the interval <m>(-\infty,$cp1 )</m>, we could pick the point <m>x=$auxpoint1 </m>.  <m>f'($auxpoint1 ) = $fun_prime_a1 
<conditionalContent>
<case condition="$fun_prime_a1 > 0">
\gt 
</case>
<else>
\lt 
</else>
</conditionalContent>
 0</m>.  <m>f(x)</m> is
<conditionalContent>
<case condition="$fun_prime_a1 > 0">
increasing
</case>
<else>
decreasing
</else>
</conditionalContent>
 on the interval <m>(-\infty,$cp1 )</m>.</p>

<p>For the interval <m>($cp1 ,$cp2 )</m>, we could pick the point <m>x=$auxpoint2[1][1] </m>.  <m>f'($auxpoint2[1][1] ) = $fun_prime_a2 
<conditionalContent>
<case condition="$fun_prime_a2 > 0">
\gt 
</case>
<else>
\lt 
</else>
</conditionalContent>
 0</m>.  <m>f(x)</m> is
<conditionalContent>
<case condition="$fun_prime_a2 > 0">
increasing
</case>
<else>
decreasing
</else>
</conditionalContent>
 on the interval <m>($cp1 ,$cp2 )</m>.</p>

<p>For the interval <m>($cp2 ,\infty)</m>, we could pick the point <m>x=$auxpoint3 </m>.  <m>f'($auxpoint3 ) = $fun_prime_a3 
<conditionalContent>
<case condition="$fun_prime_a3 > 0">
\gt 
</case>
<else>
\lt 
</else>
</conditionalContent>
 0</m>.  <m>f(x)</m> is
<conditionalContent>
<case condition="$fun_prime_a3 > 0">
increasing
</case>
<else>
decreasing
</else>
</conditionalContent>
 on the interval <m>($cp2 ,\infty)</m>.</p>

<p>(Since the exponential is always positive, you can take a shortcut on all the above answers and just calculate the sign of the quadratic <m><math extend="$dcubic.formula" /></m> at each auxiliary point.)</p>
</exercise>

</solution>


</problem>